            </a>
            <a href="/how-to-winterize-a-pressure-washer/" class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 hover:shadow-md transition-shadow no-underline group">
              <p class="text-[#16A34A] font-black text-xs uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Care</p>
              <h3 class="text-gray-900 font-bold text-lg group-hover:text-[#1E90FF] transition-colors">How to Winterize</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm mt-2">Why a machine comes back weak in spring.</p>
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Check the water side first, in order: spigot fully open, a 5/8 or 3/4-inch hose no longer than about 25 feet, no kinks, and a clean inlet screen. Those four account for most low-pressure complaints, they take two minutes, and every one of them is free. Plenty of working machines get replaced each year for a blocked screen nobody knew existed.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Let the symptom guide you. Pulsing means a starved supply and cavitation, which gets worse the longer you work through it. Weak but steady usually means the wrong, worn or blocked nozzle. Pressure that fades twenty minutes into a session is a hot universal motor rather than a pump fault, and it recovers after a break.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The pump belongs at the bottom of the list, not the top. This is a water-supply category with an electrical component, and genuine pump damage almost always traces back to a preventable habit: running in bypass, feeding it from a starved supply, or leaving water in it over a freeze.
          </p>
        </div>


